            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Muscle group</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Flat walk</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Run</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Incline walk</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Backwards walk</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Quadriceps</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Light</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Moderate to high</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">High</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Hamstrings</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Light</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Moderate to high</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Light</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Glutes</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Light (stabilising)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Moderate to high</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Light to moderate</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Calves</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">High</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">High</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">High</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Hip flexors</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">High</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Light</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Core (stabilising)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Light</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Heart and lungs</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Light to moderate</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">High</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Moderate to high</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Two patterns stand out. The calves are busy in every mode, which is why they are
            usually the first muscles to complain when someone increases speed or grade too
            quickly. And the heart and lungs row has the biggest numbers in practice: the
            cardiovascular demand is what changes most between a slow walk and a run, far more
            than any single muscle's share.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="core-and-arms" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Does a Treadmill Work Your Core or Arms?</h2>
<p>
            The core, a little; the arms, hardly at all. The trunk muscles work throughout walking
            and running to keep you upright and to control the twisting that comes from swinging
            arms and legs in opposition. That is real work, more so when running or climbing, but
            it is stabilising work at a low level, not the kind of challenge that strengthens the
            core much. A treadmill is not an ab workout.
          </p>
          <p>
            The arms swing naturally, which helps balance and rhythm, and the shoulders do a
            little to keep that swing going. That is all. There is no meaningful resistance for
            the chest, back, shoulders or arms. Carrying light hand weights while walking is
            sometimes suggested to change that, but it adds little for the arms and can alter your
            gait and posture, so it is not something we would recommend as a substitute for
            upper-body training.
          </p>
          <p>
            Holding the handrails changes the picture in an unhelpful way. Gripping the rails or
            the console takes weight off the legs, reduces the work the core does to keep you
            balanced, and shifts the posture forward or back. Light touch for balance is fine,
            especially when learning; holding on throughout a session reduces both the muscular
            and cardiovascular benefit while the console carries on as if nothing had changed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="whole-body" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. What Does a Treadmill Do to Your Body?</h2>
<p>
            Most of what a treadmill does is cardiovascular and metabolic rather than muscular.
            Used regularly, at an effort that raises your breathing and heart rate, it does the
            things sustained aerobic exercise is generally recognised to do:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Heart and lungs.</strong> Regular aerobic exercise trains the heart to pump
            more efficiently and improves how well the body delivers and uses oxygen. Over weeks,
            the same speed and grade start to feel easier — the clearest sign of improving
            fitness.</li>
            <li><strong>Energy use.</strong> Every session costs energy, more with speed, grade and
            body weight. That can support weight management, though diet usually matters more for
            the scale.</li>
            <li><strong>Weight-bearing load.</strong> Walking and running are weight-bearing, which
            is commonly recommended for bone health, unlike cycling or swimming.</li>
            <li><strong>Leg endurance.</strong> The legs get better at working for long periods —
            useful for stairs, hills and long days on your feet.</li>
            <li><strong>Mood and sleep.</strong> Many people report feeling better, calmer or
            sleeping more soundly with regular exercise; individual experience varies.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            It can also do things you do not want if it is overdone or done badly: sore calves and
            shins from increasing too fast, aches from poor form or holding the rails, and overuse
            injuries from repeating the same hard session every day. The pattern that avoids most
            of this is gradual progression, varied sessions and rest days.
          </p>
          <p>
            For a fuller look at each of these — with the downsides stated honestly — see our page
            on the
            <a href="/benefits-of-treadmill-exercise/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">benefits of treadmill exercise</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="build-muscle" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Will a Treadmill Build Muscle or Strength?</h2>
<p>
            Not much, for most people. Muscle growth and meaningful strength gains generally come
            from working a muscle hard against resistance that increases over time. Walking and
            running use the legs many thousands of times at a light load per step, which builds
            endurance — the ability to keep going — rather than size or maximum strength.
          </p>
          <p>
            There are partial exceptions. People who have been very inactive may find their legs
            become noticeably stronger in the first weeks of regular walking, especially uphill,
            because almost any new demand produces some change from a low base. Steep incline
            work and sprinting are harder on the muscles than steady walking and can add a little
            more stimulus. But in each case the gains tend to level off quickly, because the
            treadmill cannot keep adding resistance the way a barbell or a resistance machine can.
          </p>
          <p>
            Running can also feel as though it is changing the legs' shape, and for many people
            it does — mostly through fat loss and a modest firming of muscle rather than growth.
            Distance runners are a useful illustration: their legs are very well conditioned, but
            the build is generally lean rather than muscular, because the training rewards
            endurance and economy rather than size.
          </p>
          <p>
            None of this is a criticism of the treadmill. It is excellent at what it is for. It
            simply is not a strength-training tool, and expecting it to be one leads to
            disappointment.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-it-misses" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. What Does a Treadmill Not Train, and What Should You Add?</h2>
<p>
            A treadmill leaves three gaps: the upper body, heavy lower-body strength, and the
            balance and agility that varied ground and other sports demand. For a rounded routine,
            each needs something added.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Upper body.</strong> Push and pull movements — press-ups or presses, rows,
            pull-downs or pull-ups — cover the chest, back, shoulders and arms. Bands or dumbbells
            at home are enough to start.</li>
            <li><strong>Lower-body strength.</strong> Squats, lunges, step-ups, hip hinges and
            glute bridges, with load that rises over time, build the strength and size the
            treadmill cannot. Two sessions a week is a common starting point.</li>
            <li><strong>Core strength.</strong> Planks, carries and anti-rotation exercises do far
            more for the trunk than the stabilising work of walking or running.</li>
            <li><strong>Balance and variety.</strong> Walking or running outdoors on uneven ground,
            or simple balance work, covers what the perfectly even belt cannot.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Public health guidance in many countries recommends that adults combine regular
            aerobic activity with muscle-strengthening activity on at least two days a week. A
            treadmill handles the first part very well. If you train both on the same day, the
            order matters; our page on
            <a href="/treadmill-before-or-after-weights/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill before or after weights</a>
            explains how to sequence them for your main goal. If you are new to strength training,
            a qualified coach or physical therapist can help with technique and a sensible
            starting load.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What muscles does the treadmill work?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mainly the quadriceps, hamstrings, glutes, calves and hip flexors, with the core stabilising the trunk. Flat walking leans on the calves and hip flexors; running works everything harder; incline shifts work to the glutes, hamstrings and calves; walking backwards emphasises the quadriceps. The biggest training effect is on the heart and lungs rather than the muscles.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does a treadmill do to your body?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Used regularly, it trains your heart and lungs, raises daily energy use, provides weight-bearing exercise, and builds leg endurance. Many people also report better mood and sleep. It does little for the upper body or for strength, and overdoing it can cause sore calves, shins and overuse injuries, so build up gradually and vary sessions.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a treadmill build leg muscle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not much for most people. It builds leg endurance through light, repeated effort. Very inactive people may gain some strength in the first weeks, especially with incline, but the gains level off because the treadmill cannot keep adding resistance. Squats, lunges, hinges and step-ups with increasing load build leg muscle far more effectively.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does walking on a treadmill tone your legs?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can firm them modestly, mainly by conditioning the muscles and contributing to fat loss over time. Toning is really a combination of a bit more muscle and a bit less fat, and walking contributes a little to both. Adding incline and pairing walking with strength training and sensible eating produces more visible change.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a treadmill work your abs?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only lightly. The core works throughout walking and running to keep you upright and control twisting, more so when running or climbing, but this is low-level stabilising work. It will not strengthen the abdominal muscles much. Planks, carries and anti-rotation exercises do far more, and visible abs depend mainly on body fat.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is running or walking better for working muscles on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Running works the leg muscles harder, particularly the calves, quadriceps and hip flexors, and raises heart rate more. Incline walking can match or exceed running for glute and hamstring work while keeping impact low. Neither builds much strength. Choose based on your fitness, joints and goals, and add strength training for muscle.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes About Treadmill Muscles</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most of the mistakes come from expecting a cardio machine to do a strength machine’s job, or from habits that take the legs out of the work.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the treadmill as a complete workout</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A treadmill trains the heart, lungs and leg endurance very well, but leaves the upper body untouched and the legs without heavy resistance. Relying on it alone leaves gaps in strength. Add two short strength sessions a week covering pushing, pulling, squatting and hinging, and keep the treadmill for cardio.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Holding the rails throughout</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Gripping the handrails or console takes weight off the legs, reduces the core work of balancing, and distorts posture, so fewer muscles do less work than the session suggests. Use a light touch for balance when learning, then let go. If you cannot walk the setting hands-free, lower the speed or grade.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ramping up speed or grade too quickly</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The calves and Achilles are busy in every treadmill mode, and they are usually first to complain when speed or incline rises fast. Increase one variable at a time by small steps over weeks, and treat persistent pain in the calf, shin or back of the heel as a reason to ease off.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting incline walking to build big glutes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Incline does shift work to the glutes and hamstrings, but it remains light, repeated effort. People hoping for noticeably bigger glutes from walking alone are usually disappointed. Use incline walking for conditioning and pair it with hip thrusts, squats, lunges and hinges with load that increases over time.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

